QB.add(33,[{"n":1,"q":"A 28-year-old woman presents to the gastroenterology clinic with complaints of chronic abdominal pain, diarrhoea, and weight loss over the past several months. She reports experiencing frequent episodes of diarrhoea, often with mucus and blood. A colonoscopy was performed, which showed multiple aphthous ulcers scattered throughout the terminal ileum and proximal colon. A biopsy was done, which is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Ulcerative colitis"},{"t":"Irritable bowel syndrome"},{"t":"Crohn’s disease"},{"t":"Diverticulitis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The given clinical features, along with colonoscopy findings of aphthous ulcers and biopsy findings of submucosal lymphoid aggregates and noncaseating granulomas , suggest Crohn’s disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Crohn’s disease is a chronic inflammatory condition that primarily affects the gastrointestinal system. It can involve any part from the mouth to the anus, sparing the rectum. It is one of the two main forms of inflammatory bowel disease , the other being ulcerative colitis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It most commonly affects the terminal ileum , resulting in ileocolitis. The patient presents with a chronic history of recurrent episodes of right lower quadrant pain and diarrhoea with blood or mucus . The pain is relieved on defecation . Systemic symptoms such as fever, weight loss, and lymphadenopathy may also be present."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Endoscopic/gross findings Microscopic findings"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aphthous ulcers ( earliest finding ): May progress, and Abundant neutrophils infiltrate and damage the crypt multiple lesions often coalesce into elongated, epithelium. serpentine ulcers oriented along the axis of the bowel. Submucosal lymphoid aggregates may also be seen."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Edema and loss of normal mucosal folds. Clusters of neutrophils within a crypt are referred to as"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"crypt abscesses and are often associated with crypt destruction."},{"k":"img","v":6389},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Epithelial metaplasia: Pseudopyloric metaplasia. Paneth cell metaplasia. colitis biopsy\" data-author=\"CoRus13\" data-hash=\"9406\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 4.0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cr ohn%27s_colitis_-_biopsy,_intermed._mag.jpg\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"280\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"BtfnPAvd1744376295.png\" width=\"417\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cobblestone appearance of the mucosa: Occurs due to ulceration with sparing of interspersed mucosa, a result of the patchy distribution of lesions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fissure development is common and may extend deeply Non-caseating granulomas are a hallmark of Crohn’s to become fistula tracts or sites of perforation. disease and occur in areas of active disease or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"uninvolved regions within any layer of the intestinal wall."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The intestinal wall is thickened and rubbery as a consequence of transmural oedema, inflammation, submucosal fibrosis, and hypertrophy of the muscular propria, all of which contribute to stricture formation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In cases with extensive transmural disease, mesenteric adipose tissue frequently extends over the serosal surface, known as creeping fat."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ulcerative colitis (Option A) also presents with lower abdominal pain, bloody diarrhea, and weight loss. The endoscopic and microscopic findings are mentioned in the table below."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Endoscopic findings Microscopic findings"},{"k":"img","v":6395},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mucosal and submucosal superficial involvement. Pseudopolyps. Collar button ulcers. Backwash ileitis. colitis endoscopic image\" data-author=\"Samir\" data-hash=\"9409\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 3.0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ul cerative_colitis.jpg\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"342\""},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Us3tz9t1744376443.png\" width=\"395\" /> {{caption_text}} Inflammatory infiltrates."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cryptitis and crypt abscesses. Absence of non-caseating granulomas."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) (Option B) is a functional gastrointestinal disorder characterized by abdominal pain or discomfort associated with altered bowel habits. It does not show structural changes or inflammation on endoscopy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diverticulitis(Option D) is inflammation or infection of small pouches (diverticula) that can develop in the lining of the colon. It can cause symptoms similar to Crohn’s disease. Colonoscopy may reveal inflamed or perforated diverticula, focal areas of inflammation, and, in severe cases, fistulas or strictures."}],"qi":[6379]},{"n":2,"q":"A 32-year-old female presents to the gastroenterology clinic with a three-month history of bloody diarrhoea, abdominal pain, and weight loss. She reports passing loose stools up to 10 times per day and has noticed blood in her stool intermittently. A colonoscopy was done, which showed continuous involvement and mucosal erythema of the rectum with pseudopolyps and barium enema, and a lead pipe appearance was found. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Crohn’s disease"},{"t":"Shigellosis"},{"t":"Ulcerative colitis"},{"t":"Ischemic colitis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation of bloody diarrhoea, abdominal pain, weight loss, fresh blood per rectum, colonoscopy findings of rectal involvement , and pseudopolyps and barium enema finding of lead pipe appearance is suggestive of ulcerative colitis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a type of chronic inflammatory bowel disease characterized by the involvement of the rectum/rectosigmoid junction in most cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"There is inflammation of the rectum , causing frequent bloody diarrhoea, lower abdominal pain, fatigue, and weight loss."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Retrograde inflammation can occur, 2-3 cm into the terminal ileum, causing backwash ileitis . 20% of the cases can have pancolitis (the entire colon is involved)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Endoscopic findings Microscopic findings"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The colonic mucosa may be slightly red and granular or Similar to Crohn’s disease. They include inflammatory have extensive, broad-based ulcers. Fine mucosal infiltrates , crypt abscesses , crypt distortion, and granularity can be seen. pseudo pyloric epithelial metaplasia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Isolated islands of regenerating mucosa often bulge into Granulomas are absent in ulcerative colitis the lumen (differentiating it from Crohn’s disease). to create pseudopolyps , and the tips of these polyps may fuse to create mucosal bridges."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic disease may lead to mucosal atrophy with a The diffuse inflammation is generally limited to the smooth mucosal surface that lacks normal folds. mucosa and superficial submucosa."},{"k":"img","v":6401},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ulcerative colitis is not transmural . As a result, the colon wall is not thickened, the serosal surface is normal, and strictures do not occur."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"In severe cases, extensive mucosal destruction may be accompanied by ulcers that extend into the submucosa, but the muscularis propria is rarely involved."},{"k":"img","v":6406},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Collar button ulcers are seen. They are small, deep ulcers where the button corresponds to the eroded ulcer base and the button neck corresponds to the undermined and preserved mucosa."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Crohn’s disease (Option A) also presents with lower abdominal pain, diarrhoea with blood or mucus, fatigue, and weight loss. Its endoscopic and microscopic findings are mentioned below."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Endoscopic findings Microscopic findings"},{"k":"img","v":6389},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Inflammatory infiltrates. Crypt abscesses. Epithelial metaplasia. Non-caseating granulomas. colitis biopsy\" data-author=\"CoRus13\" data-hash=\"9422\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 4.0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cr ohn%27s_colitis_-_biopsy,_intermed._mag.jpg\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"333\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Yywrp5L1744377472.png\" width=\"498\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aphthous ulcers. {{caption_text}} Transmural and segmental involvement. Skip lesions. Cobblestone appearance. Longitudinal ulcers. Fistulas and strictures (severe cases). Creeping fat (in severe cases)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Shigellosis (Option B) is an infectious disease caused by diarrhoea. It presents with fever, abdominal cramps, bloody diarrhoea, and tenesmus. While it can clinically resemble ulcerative colitis, it is acute in onset and associated with the intake of contaminated food or water. Endoscopic findings in shigellosis may include colonic inflammation and ulceration."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ischemic colitis (Option D) is a condition caused by insufficient blood flow to the colon, leading to inflammation and injury. It typically presents with abdominal pain and bloody diarrhoea, often in older adults with vascular risk factors. Endoscopic findings may include segmental or patchy areas of mucosal inflammation or ulceration, often affecting the watershed areas of the colon."}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 50-year-old male diagnosed with ulcerative colitis presents with 2-3 episodes of bloody stools per day. Which of the following drugs can be used for management?","o":[{"t":"5-aminosalicylic acid"},{"t":"Azathioprine"},{"t":"Infliximab"},{"t":"Corticosteroids"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) plays a crucial role in the management of ulcerative colitis (UC), a form of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that primarily affects the colon and rectum."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Routes of Administration:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rectal administration: For patients with mild to moderate UC limited to the rectum or distal colon, 5-ASA is available in rectal formulations such as suppositories or enemas. These formulations allow direct delivery of the medication to the inflamed areas, providing targeted therapy and faster symptom relief, but compliance is low."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Oral administration: For more extensive disease involvement or when rectal administration alone is insufficient, oral formulations of 5-ASA are used."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Inducing remission : During active flares of UC, 5-ASA is often used to induce remission. It helps reduce inflammation in the colon and rectum, thereby alleviating symptoms such as diarrhoea, rectal bleeding, abdominal pain, and urgency."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Maintenance therapy : Once remission is achieved, 5-ASA is often continued as maintenance therapy to prevent relapse and maintain long-term remission. Long-term use of 5-ASA has been shown to reduce the frequency and severity of flares in patients with UC."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Combination therapy: In some cases, 5-ASA may be used in combination with other medications such as corticosteroids (Option D) , immunomodulators, or biologic agents, particularly in patients with moderate to severe UC who do not respond adequately to 5-ASA alone. Anti-TNF alpha inhibitors (infliximab (Option C), adalimumab) can be used as anti-inflammatories."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Azathioprine (Option B) is used as a steroid-sparing agent."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Safety profile: 5-ASA is generally well-tolerated, with the most common adverse effects being gastrointestinal symptoms such as nausea, abdominal pain, and diarrhoea . Serious adverse effects may include allergic reactions, pancreatitis, or kidney problems."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Surgery is recommended for patients who do not improve after medical treatment or with toxic megacolon and perforation. Indications for elective surgery include:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Failure of medical therapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Growth retardation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extraintestinal disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Malignant change."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 35-year-old man presented with bloody diarrhoea, abdominal pain and tenesmus for the past two weeks. On colonoscopy, pseudopolyps were noted in the colon. Which of the following drugs can be used in this condition?","o":[{"t":"Sulphadoxine"},{"t":"Sulfamethoxazole"},{"t":"Sulfinpyrazone"},{"t":"Sulfasalazine"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bloody diarrhoea, abdominal pain, tenesmus, and pseudopolyps are suggestive of ulcerative colitis. Sulfasalazine is commonly used in the treatment of ulcerative colitis (inflammatory bowel disease)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti-inflammatory Properties: Sulfasalazine is composed of two components: sulfapyridine and 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) . The 5-ASA component is responsible for the drug's anti-inflammatory effects. It inhibits the production of inflammatory mediators such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes, which play a role in the inflammation seen in ulcerative colitis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immunomodulatory Effects: Sulfasalazine also has immunomodulatory effects, which help to regulate the immune response in the gastrointestinal tract. By modulating the activity of immune cells such as macrophages and T cells, sulfasalazine can reduce inflammation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Antibacterial Activity: Sulfasalazine's sulfapyridine component has antibacterial properties. It acts by disrupting the metabolism of certain bacteria, particularly those in the gut microbiota. By targeting bacteria, sulfasalazine may help to restore balance to the gut microbiome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Local Action in the Colon: Sulfasalazine is administered orally, and its 5-ASA component is not absorbed systemically to a significant extent. Instead, it remains largely in the colon, where it exerts its anti-inflammatory effects directly on the inflamed tissue. This targeted action reduces the risk of systemic side effects commonly associated with corticosteroids or other systemic immunosuppressive agents."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Maintenance Therapy: Sulfasalazine is often used as maintenance therapy in ulcerative colitis. It helps prevent flare-ups and maintain remission over the long term."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic Relief: In addition to its disease-modifying effects, sulfasalazine can provide symptomatic relief from the symptoms of ulcerative colitis, such as diarrhoea, abdominal pain, and rectal bleeding."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 28-year-old female presents with recurrent abdominal pain, diarrhoea, and weight loss. She has a history of Crohn's disease confirmed by colonoscopy and biopsy. Which of the following dietary recommendations is most commonly advised for Crohn's disease management?","o":[{"t":"Increasing intake of high-fiber foodsB"},{"t":"Avoiding foods high in fat and sugar"},{"t":"Incorporating probiotics into the diet"},{"t":"Drinking caffeinated beverages in moderation"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Probiotics can help restore a healthy balance of gut bacteria and reduce inflammation in Crohn's disease patients, contributing to symptom improvement. They are most commonly advised as a first-line non-pharmacological treatment in the management of Crohn's disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dietary advice for Crohn's disease:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increasing intake of high-fiber foods (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"While fibre is generally recommended for digestive health, Crohn's disease patients may need to limit high-fibre foods during flare-ups to avoid exacerbating symptoms."}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 28-year-old female presents with recurrent and persistent abdominal pain, diarrhoea, and weight loss. She has a history of Crohn's disease confirmed by colonoscopy and biopsy. There is no fistulization present on colonoscopy. Despite initial treatment with mesalamine, her symptoms have not improved significantly. Which of the following treatment options is most appropriate as a next step in managing her Crohn's disease?","o":[{"t":"CorticosteroidsB"},{"t":"Azathioprine"},{"t":"Infliximab"},{"t":"Methotrexate"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In Crohn's disease management, treatment escalation is often necessary when initial therapies like mesalamine fail to adequately control symptoms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti-TNF therapy, such as infliximab, is a recommended next step for patients with moderate to severe Crohn's disease who do not respond to conventional medications like mesalamine."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti-TNF agents target tumour necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha), an inflammatory cytokine involved in Crohn's disease pathogenesis, and can induce and maintain remission in affected individuals."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A step-wise approach to treatment strategies for Crohn's disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Start with 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) medications like mesalamine. These can help reduce inflammation in the gut."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For patients who don't respond well to corticosteroids or need longer-term treatment, immunomodulators like azathioprine, mercaptopurine, or methotrexate may be used. These medications work by suppressing the immune system to reduce inflammation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biological agents, such as anti-TNF drugs like infliximab, adalimumab, or certolizumab, are often considered for patients with moderate to severe Crohn's disease who haven't responded to other treatments. These drugs target specific proteins in the immune system to reduce inflammation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In some cases, a combination of medications may be used to achieve better control of symptoms and reduce the risk of complications. For example, combining an immunomodulator with a biological agent may be effective for certain patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nutritional therapy, including enteral nutrition (liquid diets) or specific dietary modifications, may be recommended as adjunctive therapy or for maintenance of remission in some patients, especially children or those with specific nutritional needs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Surgery may be necessary for complications of Crohn's disease, such as strictures, fistulas, or abscesses that don't respond to medical treatment. Surgery aims to remove affected portions of the intestine or repair damaged tissues."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Corticosteroids (Option A), like prednisone, are potent anti-inflammatory medications used in Crohn's disease, especially during flare-ups or when rapid symptom relief is needed. However, they are typically reserved for short-term use due to potential side effects with long-term use, such as weight gain, bone density loss, and increased infection risk."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Azathioprine (Option B) is an immunosuppressive medication often used as an adjunctive therapy for Crohn's disease. Azathioprine is particularly beneficial for patients who require maintenance therapy to prevent disease flares and helps to maintain remission."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Methotrexate (Option D) is another immunosuppressive agent used in Crohn's disease management, especially in cases where other medications have not been effective or are not tolerated. It can help reduce inflammation and control symptoms, particularly in patients with fistulizing or refractory disease."}]},{"n":7,"q":"Which medication, recently approved by the FDA for treating Crohn's disease, is a selective Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitor?","o":[{"t":"Vedolizumab"},{"t":"Infliximab"},{"t":"Ustekinumab"},{"t":"Upadacitinib"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"of moderate to severe Crohn's disease in patients who have not responded adequately to conventional therapies. It works by targeting specific signalling pathways involved in inflammation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It selectively inhibits JAK1, a key enzyme involved in signalling pathways related to inflammation and immune response. By targeting JAK1, upadacitinib helps modulate the immune system's activity, reduce inflammation, and improve symptoms in Crohn's disease patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Novel therapies approved by FDA for Crohn's Disease:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Medication Mechanism of Action Common Side Effects"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Vedolizum Integrin receptor antagonist; blocks Infusion reactions, headache, ab leukocyte trafficking respiratory infections"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ustekinum Targets IL-12 and IL-23 cytokines Upper respiratory infections, ab involved in inflammation headache, fatigue"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tofacitinib Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitor Upper respiratory tract infections,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"modulates immune response headache, diarrhoea"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Upadacitin Selective JAK1 inhibitor; reduces Upper respiratory tract infections, ib inflammation nausea, elevated liver enzymes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Risankizu IL-23 inhibitor targets inflammation Upper respiratory tract infections, mab pathways headache, nausea"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Vedolizumab (Option A) is an integrin receptor antagonist that blocks leukocyte trafficking and is used in Crohn's disease treatment, but it is not a JAK inhibitor."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Infliximab (Option B) is a tumour necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha) inhibitor, not a JAK inhibitor, and has been used in Crohn's disease management for some time."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ustekinumab (Option C) targets interleukin (IL)-12 and IL-23 cytokines and was approved for treating Crohn's disease."}]}]);
QB.imgs({"6401":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6406":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6379":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6389":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6395":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});